{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"8aaceaae","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","papermill":{"duration":0.00545,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.818102+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.812652+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Tokenization\n","\n","Tokenization is one of the most fundamental concepts in Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Large Language Models (LLMs).\n","\n","Before an LLM such as GPT, Gemini, or Claude can understand and process your prompt, it must convert your text into a sequence of tokens.\n","\n","A token can represent:\n","\n","- An entire word.\n","- Part of a word.\n","- A single character.\n","- Punctuation.\n","- Whitespace or other text fragments.\n","\n","The most important thing to understand is that an LLM does not directly process human readable sentences. It processes numerical representations of tokens.\n","\n","Tokenization is the process of breaking text into smaller units called tokens.\n","\n","For example, consider this sentence:\n","\n","```\n","I love Machine Learning.\n","```\n","\n","A simple word-based tokenizer might produce:\n","\n","```\n","[\"I\", \"love\", \"Machine\", \"Learning\", \".\"]\n","```\n","\n","Another tokenizer might produce:\n","\n","```\n","[\"I\", \" love\", \" Machine\", \" Learning\", \".\"]\n","```\n","\n","Notice that the second tokenizer preserves spaces as part of tokens.\n","\n","A subword tokenizer might produce something like:\n","\n","```\n","[\"I\", \" love\", \" Machine\", \" Learning\", \".\"]\n","```\n","\n","For another sentence:\n","\n","```\n","Tokenization is important.\n","```\n","\n","A tokenizer could produce:\n","\n","```\n","[\"Token\", \"ization\", \" is\", \" important\", \".\"]\n","```\n","\n","Here, `Tokenization` is divided into two pieces:\n","\n","- `Token`\n","- `ization`\n","\n","Why?\n","\n","Because modern LLMs commonly use subword tokenization. They don't necessarily need a separate vocabulary entry for every possible word.\n","\n","Important: These are illustrative tokenizations. Actual token boundaries depend on the tokenizer used by the particular model.\n","\n","**Is tokenization case-sensitive?**\n","\n","| Tokenizer | Case-sensitive? |\n","|---|---|\n","| bert-base-uncased | No |\n","| bert-base-cased | Yes |\n","| GPT-2 | Yes |\n","\n","## Why do we need tokenization?\n","\n","Imagine you send this prompt to an LLM:\n","\n","```\n","What is Artificial Intelligence?\n","```\n","\n","A computer does not inherently understand the meaning of this English sentence.\n","\n","It needs a numerical representation.\n","\n","The process looks like this:\n","\n","1. Human-readable input: `What is Artificial Intelligence?`\n","2. Tokenization\n","    - Tokens: `[\"What\", \" is\", \" Artificial\", \" Intelligence\", \"?\"]`\n","3. Token-to-ID conversion\n","    - Token IDs: `[2061, 318, 9047, 9344, 30]`\n","    - Illustrative IDs, not actual IDs from a specific model.\n","4. Embedding layer\n","5. Numerical vectors: Each token ID is mapped to an embedding vector.\n","6. Transformer model: Attention, neural network layers, and prediction.\n","\n","Why is this useful?\n","\n","1. Computers can efficiently process numerical data.\n","2. Tokenization creates a manageable vocabulary.\n","3. Subword tokenization handles unfamiliar words.\n","4. It allows the model to learn patterns between language units.\n","5. It makes language processing compatible with neural networks.\n","\n","## Different types of tokenization\n","\n","There are several tokenization techniques. Let's understand each one.\n","\n","### Word based tokenization\n","\n","This method divides text into individual words.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"51bdbdb5","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.829754Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.829285Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.838587Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.837484Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.018179,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.841078+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.822899+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["text = \"I love Machine Learning.\""]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":2,"id":"399d3e04","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.852146Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.851579Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.64212Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.640707Z"},"papermill":{"duration":4.798846,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.644344+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:39.845498+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["[nltk_data] Downloading package punkt to /usr/share/nltk_data...\n","[nltk_data]   Package punkt is already up-to-date!\n"]},{"data":{"text/plain":["True"]},"execution_count":2,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["import nltk\n","from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\n","\n","nltk.download('punkt')"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":3,"id":"74a0cbbe","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.655721Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.654934Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.694549Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.693198Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.048012,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.697091+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.649079+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["['I', 'love', 'Machine', 'Learning', '.']\n"]}],"source":["tokens = word_tokenize(text)\n","print(tokens)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"8c68f80b","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.192008Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.191621Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.200014Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.198793Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.19198Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.004521,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.706102+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.701581+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**How does it work?**\n","\n","The tokenizer identifies spaces and punctuation and splits the sentence accordingly.\n","\n","**Advantages:**\n","\n","- Easy to implement.\n","- Easy to understand.\n","- Words remain meaningful units.\n","\n","**Disadvantages:**\n","\n","- Very large vocabulary.\n","- Cannot efficiently handle unseen words.\n","- Different word forms may require separate vocabulary entries.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","play\n","playing\n","played\n","player\n","```\n","\n","A word-based tokenizer may need four separate vocabulary entries.\n","\n","This becomes problematic when working with millions of possible words.\n","\n","### Character-based tokenization\n","\n","Instead of splitting text into words, this method splits it into individual characters."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":4,"id":"5a7d396c","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.716754Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.716395Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.721538Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.72058Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.013058,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.72352+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.710462+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["['I', ' ', 'l', 'o', 'v', 'e', ' ', 'M', 'a', 'c', 'h', 'i', 'n', 'e', ' ', 'L', 'e', 'a', 'r', 'n', 'i', 'n', 'g', '.']\n"]}],"source":["tokens = list(text)\n","print(tokens)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"829a2f47","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.004606,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.732778+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.728172+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Advantages:**\n","\n","- Very small vocabulary.\n","- Can represent almost any word.\n","- Handles unfamiliar words naturally.\n","\n","**Disadvantages:**\n","\n","- Creates many more tokens.\n","- The model needs to learn word-level patterns from individual characters.\n","- Longer sequences increase computational costs.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","Artificial Intelligence\n","```\n","\n","A character-based tokenizer produces many more tokens than a word-based tokenizer.\n","\n","### Subword tokenization\n","\n","Very important for LLMs\n","\n","Subword tokenization divides text into meaningful or frequently occurring smaller pieces."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":5,"id":"239546e3","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.743841Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.743483Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.884099Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.883071Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.14942,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.886839+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.737419+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\n","\n","\n","Encoder: Encoding(num_tokens=5, attributes=[ids, type_ids, tokens, offsets, attention_mask, special_tokens_mask, overflowing])\n","Original text: I love Machine Learning.\n","Tokens: ['I', 'love', 'Machine', 'Learning', '[UNK]']\n","Token IDs: [1, 40, 62, 61, 0]\n","\n","Decoded text: I love Machine Learning\n"]}],"source":["from tokenizers import Tokenizer\n","from tokenizers.models import BPE\n","from tokenizers.trainers import BpeTrainer\n","from tokenizers.pre_tokenizers import Whitespace\n","\n","tokenizer = Tokenizer(BPE(unk_token=\"[UNK]\"))\n","\n","trainer = BpeTrainer(\n","    vocab_size=100,\n","    special_tokens=[\"[UNK]\"]\n",")\n","\n","tokenizer.pre_tokenizer = Whitespace()\n","\n","texts = [\n","    \"I love natural language processing\",\n","    \"Natural language processing is interesting\",\n","    \"I love Machine Learning\"\n","]\n","\n","tokenizer.train_from_iterator(texts, trainer)\n","\n","output = tokenizer.encode(text)\n","\n","# Display results\n","print(\"Encoder:\", output)\n","print(\"Original text:\", text)\n","print(\"Tokens:\", output.tokens)\n","print(\"Token IDs:\", output.ids)\n","\n","# Decode token IDs back into text\n","print(\"\\nDecoded text:\", tokenizer.decode(output.ids))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"efd664cb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.004408,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.895731+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.891323+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["The exact divisions depend on the tokenizer.\n","\n","Why is subword tokenization so popular?\n","\n","Consider these words:\n","\n","```\n","play\n","playing\n","played\n","player\n","playful\n","```\n","\n","A subword tokenizer can reuse the common piece `play` instead of requiring an entirely independent vocabulary entry for every variation.\n","\n","This provides a balance between word-based and character-based tokenization.\n","\n","### Byte-based tokenization\n","\n","Byte-based tokenization represents text using bytes, often UTF-8 bytes."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":6,"id":"c5715b86","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.907036Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.906622Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.912821Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.911812Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.01479,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.914883+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.900093+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Original Text: I love Machine Learning.\n","Byte Tokens: b'I love Machine Learning.'\n","Byte Values: [73, 32, 108, 111, 118, 101, 32, 77, 97, 99, 104, 105, 110, 101, 32, 76, 101, 97, 114, 110, 105, 110, 103, 46]\n"]}],"source":["tokens = text.encode('utf-8')\n","\n","print(\"Original Text:\", text)\n","print(\"Byte Tokens:\", tokens)\n","print(\"Byte Values:\", list(tokens))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1fdaa30f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.004559,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.92429+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.919731+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["A byte-level tokenizer can use individual bytes or merge frequently occurring byte sequences into larger tokens.\n","\n","This is particularly useful for handling:\n","\n","- Unusual words.\n","- Multiple languages.\n","- Emojis.\n","- Special characters.\n","- Previously unseen text.\n","\n","Many modern LLM tokenizers use byte-level techniques combined with subword merging.\n","\n","## How do modern LLMs tokenize text internally\n","\n","Now let's understand three important algorithms used in modern tokenizers.\n","\n","### BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)\n","\n","Important for LLM interviews\n","\n","BPE is a subword tokenization algorithm that builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent units.\n","\n","Let's understand it step by step.\n","\n","Suppose our training dataset contains:\n","\n","```\n","low\n","lower\n","lowest\n","newer\n","newest\n","```\n","\n","Step 1: Initially, divide words into characters.\n","\n","```\n","low   → l o w\n","lower → l o w e r\n","lowest → l o w e s t\n","newer → n e w e r\n","newest → n e w e s t\n","```\n","\n","Step 2: Count frequently occurring adjacent pairs.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","l + o\n","o + w\n","e + r\n","e + s\n","s + t\n","```\n","\n","Suppose `l + o` occurs most frequently.\n","\n","Step 3: Merge the most frequent pair.\n","\n","```\n","l + o → lo\n","```\n","\n","Now the vocabulary contains `lo` as a new token.\n","\n","Step 4: Repeat the process.\n","\n","Suppose `lo + w` is now the most frequent pair.\n","\n","```\n","lo + w → low\n","```\n","\n","Then another merge might be:\n","\n","```\n","e + r → er\n","```\n","\n","The algorithm keeps merging frequently occurring pairs until it reaches its target vocabulary size or another stopping condition.\n","\n","Step 5: Final tokenization\n","\n","The vocabulary may now contain:\n","\n","```\n","l\n","o\n","w\n","lo\n","low\n","er\n","est\n","new\n","...\n","```\n","\n","So the word:\n","\n","```\n","lowest\n","```\n","\n","might be tokenized as:\n","\n","```\n","[\"low\", \"est\"]\n","```\n","\n","This is a simplified demonstration. Actual BPE tokenization depends on the learned merge rules, initial alphabet, preprocessing, and tokenization configuration.\n","\n","**Where is BPE used?**\n","\n","- GPT-family tokenizers commonly use BPE variants.\n","- Many code and text language models use BPE-based tokenization.\n","\n","**`texts`**\n","\n","`texts` is your training dataset. It contains example sentences that the tokenizer learns from.\n","\n","The tokenizer analyzes these sentences to learn:\n","\n","- Which words appear frequently.\n","- Which characters or groups of characters occur together.\n","- Which subword units can be combined.\n","- How to build its vocabulary.\n","\n","For example, it might learn tokens such as:\n","\n","text\n","\n","```text\n","I\n","love\n","natural\n","language\n","processing\n","machine\n","learning\n","```\n","\n","It can also learn smaller subword pieces, depending on the training data.\n","\n","**Why do we need `texts`?**\n","\n","Because a BPE tokenizer needs examples to learn how to split text into tokens.\n","\n","Think of `texts` as the textbook from which the tokenizer learns its vocabulary.\n","\n","**What is the difference between training and encoding?**\n","\n","This is the most important concept.\n","\n","| Operation | Purpose |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| `texts` | Provides training examples |\n","| `tokenizer.train_from_iterator()` | Learns the vocabulary |\n","| `tokenizer.encode()` | Converts new text into tokens |\n","| `output.tokens` | Displays the resulting tokens |\n","| `output.ids` | Displays numerical IDs for tokens |\n","\n","**Why do we encode text?**\n","\n","Computers and machine learning models generally work with numerical representations rather than raw text.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","text\n","\n","```text\n","\"I love Machine Learning\"\n","```\n","\n","Might become:\n","\n","python\n","\n","```python\n","[12, 25, 43, 67]\n","```\n","\n","These numbers are called token IDs.\n","\n","Tokenization is important in:\n","\n","1. LLMs: Models such as GPT process text through token IDs.\n","2. Text generation: Models generate sequences of token IDs that can be decoded into text.\n","3. NLP: Used in text classification, sentiment analysis, and language translation.\n","4. Embeddings: Token IDs are converted into vector representations.\n","\n","One important thing to remember:\n","\n","- Training teaches the tokenizer how to break text into tokens.\n","- Encoding converts text into tokens and their numerical IDs.\n","- Decoding converts token IDs back into readable text.\n","\n","In simple terms, training builds the tokenizer's vocabulary, while encoding uses that vocabulary to convert sentences into numbers.\n","\n","### WordPiece\n","\n","It is associated with BERT and related models.\n","\n","Unlike basic BPE, WordPiece typically chooses merges based on how well they improve the likelihood of the training data under its vocabulary model.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","playing\n","```\n","\n","Could be divided into:\n","\n","```\n","[\"play\", \"##ing\"]\n","```\n","\n","What does `##` mean?\n","\n","It indicates that the subword is attached to the preceding token rather than beginning a new word.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","unbelievable\n","```\n","\n","Might become:\n","\n","```\n","[\"un\", \"##believ\", \"##able\"]\n","```\n","\n","The exact segmentation depends on the trained vocabulary.\n","\n","Key difference:\n","\n","- BPE: Uses frequency-based pair merging.\n","- WordPiece: Uses a likelihood-based criterion for selecting vocabulary pieces."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":7,"id":"c0273c5f","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.941816Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.94146Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:14.620497Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:14.619234Z"},"papermill":{"duration":29.687641,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:14.622784+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:43:44.935143+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["Warning: You are sending unauthenticated requests to the HF Hub. Please set a HF_TOKEN to enable higher rate limits and faster downloads.\n"]},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"8ddd00227feb4d4c86ec2170c69e0cec","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["config.json:   0%|          | 0.00/570 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"ff401f0fedea4cd5b30e8328b1839d48","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["tokenizer_config.json:   0%|          | 0.00/48.0 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"ae8dedea03d143d7a31215f2faa5b811","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["vocab.txt:   0%|          | 0.00/232k [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"bbad45bc8c824bd19017216a17b1fe4d","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["tokenizer.json:   0%|          | 0.00/466k [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["['i', 'love', 'machine', 'learning', '.']\n"]}],"source":["from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n","\n","tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n","    \"bert-base-unc