ount":14,"id":"bb97192f","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.32876Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.328365Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.360286Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.359192Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.041725,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.362386+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.320661+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":[" Token ID     Word  Dimension 1  Dimension 2  Dimension 3  Dimension 4  Dimension 5\n","        1        I     0.355715     0.794411     0.641891     0.677746     0.769253\n","       40     love     0.336720    -0.525019     1.829613    -0.672608    -2.722382\n","       62  Machine    -1.040744     0.289729    -0.903577    -0.607223     0.989188\n","       61 Learning    -1.623824     0.214850    -0.146567    -0.187914    -1.430099\n","        0    [UNK]    -0.472836     1.573009     0.022164    -1.028438     0.230858\n"]}],"source":["import pandas as pd\n","\n","# Create embedding table\n","data = embeddings.detach().numpy()\n","\n","df = pd.DataFrame(\n","    data,\n","    columns=[f\"Dimension {i+1}\" for i in range(embeddings.shape[1])]\n",")\n","\n","# Add Token ID and Word columns\n","df.insert(0, \"Token ID\", output.ids)\n","df.insert(1, \"Word\", output.tokens)\n","\n","# Display table\n","print(df.to_string(index=False))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4139a352","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005813,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.374108+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.368295+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["The model has an embedding dimension of 5 because the `embedding_dim` is set to 5, for more dimension set it that number.\n","\n","When the model receives token ID `39`, it retrieves the corresponding embedding: `[0.823700, 0.841187, -0.318946, -1.580562, -0.405868, -2.024284]`\n","\n","The corresponding word is `love`.\n","\n","**Important distinction:**\n","\n","The embedding matrix is a learnable parameter of the model.\n","\n","During training, its values are updated through backpropagation and optimization.\n","\n","The complete process:\n","\n","```\n","Text\n","  ↓\n","Tokenizer\n","  ↓\n","Tokens\n","  ↓\n","Token IDs\n","  ↓\n","Embedding lookup\n","  ↓\n","Embedding vectors\n","  ↓\n","Positional information\n","  ↓\n","Transformer layers\n","  ↓\n","Output logits\n","  ↓\n","Next-token prediction\n","```\n","\n","Tokenization and embedding are two different operations.\n","\n","- Tokenization determines how text is divided into tokens.\n","- Token IDs assign numerical identifiers to those tokens.\n","- Embedding converts those IDs into dense numerical vectors.\n","\n","## What happens when an LLM receives an actual prompt?\n","\n","Let's take this example:\n","\n","```\n","The cat is sleeping.\n","```\n","\n","Assume a hypothetical tokenizer produces:\n","\n","```\n","tokens = [ \"The\", \" cat\", \" is\", \" sleeping\",\".\"]\n","```\n","\n","The tokenizer then maps them to IDs:\n","\n","```\n","token_ids = [101, 205, 310, 415, 25]\n","```\n","\n","Now let's follow the complete data flow.\n","\n","### LLM tokenization pipeline\n","\n","1. Input prompt: `The cat is sleeping.`\n","2. Tokenization: `[\"The\", \" cat\", \" is\", \" sleeping\", \".\"]`\n","3. Token IDs: `[101, 205, 310, 415, 25]`\n","4. Embedding lookup: Five vectors, each with the model's embedding dimension.\n","5. Transformer\n","    - Self-attention and feed-forward layers process the vectors.\n","6. Output logits and probability distribution\n","    - The model calculates scores for possible next tokens.\n","7. Select next token\n","    - For example: ` on`\n","8. Detokenization\n","\n","Convert generated token IDs back into readable text.\n","\n","Notice that the model doesn't generate an entire sentence at once in ordinary autoregressive generation. It generally predicts one token at a time, appends it to the sequence, and repeats.\n","\n","\n","## Special tokens\n","\n","Not every token represents an ordinary word. LLMs also use special tokens to control the structure and flow of conversations.\n","\n","Examples include:\n","\n","| Special token | Purpose                                          |\n","| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n","| BOS           | Beginning of sequence                            |\n","| EOS           | End of sequence                                  |\n","| PAD           | Padding sequences                                |\n","| UNK           | Unknown token in some tokenizers                 |\n","| SEP           | Separates text segments in some models           |\n","| MASK          | Represents masked content in models such as BERT |\n","\n","Some GPT-style conversational formats use tokens such as:\n","\n","- `<|im_start|>` — starts a message.\n","- `<|im_sep|>` — separates a message header from its content.\n","- `<|im_end|>` — ends a message.\n","- `<|endoftext|>` — a special end-of-text token in certain GPT tokenizers.\n","- `<|ghissue|>` — not a standard token; avoid assuming that all models share the same special-token vocabulary.\n","\n","For example, a simplified conversational representation might look like:\n","\n","```\n","<|im_start|>user\n","What is machine learning?\n","<|im_end|>\n","\n","<|im_start|>assistant\n","Machine learning is a branch of AI...\n","<|im_end|>\n","```\n","\n","These markers help a model distinguish between different messages and their boundaries.\n","\n","Actual chat formats vary between model providers.\n","\n","## Detokenization\n","\n","Detokenization is the reverse process of tokenization.\n","\n","Tokenization:\n","\n","```\n","Human-readable text\n","       ↓\n","Tokens\n","       ↓\n","Token IDs\n","```\n","\n","Detokenization:\n","\n","```\n","Token IDs\n","       ↓\n","Tokens\n","       ↓\n","Human-readable text\n","```\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","tokens = [\"Hello\", \" world\", \"!\"]\n","```\n","\n","Detokenization produces:\n","\n","```\n","Hello world!\n","```\n","\n","One important detail: some tokenizers operate at the byte level, so decoding must correctly reconstruct UTF-8 text. You cannot always simply join token strings with spaces.\n","\n","## Relationship between tokenization and context windows\n","\n","A context window represents the maximum number of tokens a model can process in a given request, subject to the model's specific architecture and API limits.\n","\n","For example, suppose a model supports a context window of 8,192 tokens.\n","\n","Your input contains:\n","\n","```\n","System prompt:       500 tokens\n","User question:       200 tokens\n","Retrieved RAG data:  6,000 tokens\n","Conversation history: 1,000 tokens\n","```\n","\n","Total:\n","\n","$$ 500+200+6000+1000=7700 $$\n","\n","You have:\n","\n","$$ 8192-7700=492 $$\n","\n","tokens remaining.\n","\n","This is a simplified example; output-token reservations and provider-specific accounting also matter.\n","\n","Why should an AI engineer care?\n","\n","Because tokenization directly affects:\n","\n","1. Context-window usage.\n","2. API costs.\n","3. Response latency.\n","4. RAG chunk sizes.\n","5. Maximum input and output lengths.\n","6. How much conversation history you can retain.\n","\n","### Tokenization in RAG\n","\n","Imagine you retrieve 10 documents from a vector database.\n","\n","Each document contains 1,000 tokens.\n","\n","Your retrieved context contains:\n","\n","$$ 10 \\times 1000 = 10000 \\text{ tokens} $$\n","\n","If your model only has 8,192 tokens available for the entire request, you cannot simply insert all 10 documents alongside your instructions and question.\n","\n","You need to:\n","\n","- Reduce the number of retrieved documents.\n","- Use smaller chunks.\n","- Rerank the retrieved chunks.\n","- Remove redundant content.\n","- Compress or summarize retrieved information.\n","\n","This is why tokenization is important when designing RAG pipelines.\n","\n","## Why does the same sentence produce different token counts?\n","\n","Different LLMs can use different tokenizers and vocabularies.\n","\n","Consider: `Artificial Intelligence`\n","\n","- One tokenizer might produce: `[\"Artificial\", \" Intelligence\"]`\n","- Another might produce: `[\"Art\", \"ificial\", \" Intelligence\"]`\n","\n","Both are valid tokenization strategies.\n","\n","Other factors that affect token counts include:\n","\n","- Language.\n","- Special characters.\n","- Emojis.\n","- Numbers.\n","- Programming code.\n","- Whitespace.\n","- Vocabulary and tokenizer design.\n","\n","For example, Bangla text can have a different token-to-character ratio from English text.\n","\n","This is important when building multilingual applications.\n","\n","## Misconceptions\n","\n","| Misconception                               | Correct understanding                                                                   |\n","| ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| One token equals one word                   | A token can be a word, subword, character, or byte sequence.                            |\n","| Token IDs represent meaning                 | Token IDs are identifiers, not semantic values.                                         |\n","| Tokenization understands language           | Tokenization segments text; semantic representations are learned by the neural network. |\n","| All LLMs use the same tokenizer             | Different models can use different tokenizers.                                          |\n","| Tokenization and embedding are the same     | Tokenization creates tokens; embedding maps token IDs into vectors.                     |\n","| A shorter sentence always uses fewer tokens | Token count depends on the tokenizer and text composition.                              |\n","\n","## Interview questions\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is tokenization?</summary>\n","\n","Tokenization is the process of converting raw text into smaller units called tokens. These tokens are then mapped to numerical IDs, which an LLM uses as input.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do modern LLMs use subword tokenization?</summary>\n","\n","Subword tokenization balances vocabulary size and sequence length. It allows models to represent unfamiliar words by combining smaller known pieces instead of requiring every possible word in the vocabulary.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between BPE and WordPiece?</summary>\n","\n","BPE repeatedly merges frequent adjacent token pairs, whereas WordPiece generally selects vocabulary merges based on their improvement to the likelihood of the training data.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between tokenization and embedding?</summary>\n","\n","Tokenization converts text into tokens and token IDs. Embedding converts token IDs into dense numerical vectors that a neural network can process.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What happens when a model encounters an unknown word?</summary>\n","\n","A subword or byte-level tokenizer can often represent the unfamiliar word using smaller known units. Therefore, it doesn't necessarily need an unknown-token representation.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>How does tokenization affect LLM API costs?</summary>\n","\n","Many LLM APIs calculate input and output charges based on token counts. More tokens generally mean higher costs, although exact pricing depends on the provider and model.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why is tokenization important in RAG?</summary>\n","\n","It helps control the size of retrieved context, chunk sizes, context-window consumption, and API costs.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is detokenization?</summary>\n","\n","Detokenization converts token IDs back into human-readable text.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","# Positional Encoding\n","\n","Positional Encoding is a technique used in Transformer architectures to give the model information about the position or order of tokens in a sequence.\n","\n","## Why do we need positional encoding?\n","\n","Consider these two sentences:\n","\n","- Sentence A: `The cat ate the mouse.`\n","- Sentence B: `The mouse ate the cat.`\n","\n","Both sentences contain almost exactly the same words, but their meanings are completely different.\n","\n","The difference comes from the order of the words.\n","\n","Now, consider how a Transformer processes text.\n","\n","First, it converts tokens into numerical vectors using an embedding layer.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","| Token | Token embedding         |\n","| ----- | ----------------------- |\n","| The   | [0.21, 0.45, 0.12, ...] |\n","| cat   | [0.32, 0.18, 0.67, ...] |\n","| ate   | [0.51, 0.22, 0.39, ...] |\n","| mouse | [0.14, 0.71, 0.28, ...] |\n","\n","The problem is that self-attention, by itself, does not inherently know the original order of these tokens.\n","\n","Self-attention can determine relationships between tokens, but without positional information, it does not know whether `cat` appeared before or after `ate`.\n","\n","This is why we introduce positional encoding.\n","\n","It adds positional information to token embeddings so the Transformer can understand the sequence order.\n","\n","## Where does positional encoding happen in a Transformer?\n","\n","The process is:\n","\n","1. User provides text.\n","2. Tokenizer converts text into token IDs.\n","3. Embedding layer converts token IDs into vectors.\n","4. **Positional encoding** generates position vectors.\n","5. **Token embeddings and positional encodings are added together.**\n","6. The resulting vectors are passed into the Transformer layers.\n","\n","The mathematical operation is:\n","\n","$$ X = E + PE $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $X$ = final input representation.\n","- $E$ = token embedding.\n","- $PE$ = positional encoding.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","Suppose we have a token embedding:\n","\n","$$ E\\_{\\text{cat}}=[0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8] $$\n","\n","And its positional encoding:\n","\n","$$ PE_1=[0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7] $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ X\\_{\\text{cat}} = [0.3,0.7,1.1,1.5] $$\n","\n","This final vector contains both:\n","\n","- Semantic information: What does the token mean?\n","- Positional information: Where does the token appear?\n","\n","An important point: positional encoding does not replace token embeddings. It adds information to them.\n","\n","## Internal Working\n","\n","In the original Transformer architecture (Attention Is All You Need, 2017), the authors introduced Sinusoidal Positional Encoding.\n","\n","It uses mathematical functions called:\n","\n","- Sine\n","- Cosine\n","\n","These functions generate unique position-dependent patterns.\n","\n","The formulas are:\n","\n","For even dimensions:\n","\n","$$ PE(pos,2i)= \\sin\\left( \\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d\\_{model}}} \\right) $$\n","\n","For odd dimensions:\n","\n","$$ PE(pos,2i+1)= \\cos\\left( \\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d\\_{model}}} \\right) $$\n","\n","Let's understand every variable.\n","\n","| Variable         | Meaning                                       |\n","| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n","| $pos$        | Position of the token in the sequence         |\n","| $i$          | Dimension index pair                          |\n","| $d\\_{model}$ | Embedding dimension                           |\n","| $2i$         | Even dimension                                |\n","| $2i+1$       | Odd dimension                                 |\n","| 10000            | Constant controlling the range of frequencies |\n","\n","Let's break this down step by step.\n","\n","### Understanding the formula with a small example\n","\n","When $d_{model}=5$, each token embedding has 5 dimensions. Therefore, we calculate five positional encoding values for every token position.\n","\n","Suppose:\n","\n","$$\n","d_{model}=5\n","$$\n","\n","Using the original sinusoidal positional encoding formula:\n","\n","For even dimensions:\n","\n","$$\n","PE(pos,2i)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/5}}\\right)\n","$$\n","\n","For odd dimensions:\n","\n","$$\n","PE(pos,2i+1)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/5}}\\right)\n","$$\n","\n","The five dimensions are calculated as follows:\n","\n","- Dimension 0: Uses $\\sin(pos)$.\n","- Dimension 1: Uses $\\cos(pos)$.\n","- Dimension 2: Uses $\\sin(pos/10000^{2/5})=\\sin(pos/100)$.\n","- Dimension 3: Uses $\\cos(pos/10000^{2/5})=\\cos(pos/100)$.\n","- Dimension 4: Uses $\\sin(pos/10000^{4/5})=\\sin(pos/1000)$.\n","\n","Notice that dimension 4 is an even dimension, so it uses the sine function. It does not have a corresponding cosine dimension because the embedding dimension is odd.\n","\n","**Final positional encoding for $d_{model}=5$**\n","\n","$$\n","PE(pos)=\n","\\begin{bmatrix}\n","\\sin(pos)\\\\\n","\\cos(pos)\\\\\n","\\sin(pos/100)\\\\\n","\\cos(pos/100)\\\\\n","\\sin(pos/1000)\n","\\end{bmatrix}\n","$$\n","\n","### Why are there different frequencies?\n","\n","Because the Transformer needs positional patterns at different scales.\n","\n","- Higher-frequency dimensions change more quickly between positions.\n","- Lower-frequency dimensions change more slowly.\n","\n","This creates a combination of patterns that helps the Transformer distinguish different token positions.\n","\n","Important: When the embedding dimension is odd, the final dimension has no cosine partner. This is perfectly valid in the original Transformer positional encoding method.\n","\n","### How positional encoding is generated\n","\n","Let's calculate positional encodings for five positions.\n","\n","Assume:\n","\n","$$\n","d_{model}=5\n","$$\n","\n","The resulting values from our PyTorch implementation are:\n","\n","| Position | Dimension 0 | Dimension 1 | Dimension 2 | Dimension 3 | Dimension 4 |\n","| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n","| 0 | 0.000 | 1.000 | 0.000 | 1.000 | 0.000 |\n","| 1 | 0.840 | 0.540 | 0.030 | 1.000 | 0.000 |\n","| 2 | 0.910 | -0.420 | 0.050 | 1.000 | 0.000 |\n","| 3 | 0.140 | -0.990 | 0.080 | 1.000 | 0.000 |\n","| 4 | -0.760 | -0.650 | 0.100 | 0.990 | 0.000 |\n","\n","Notice something important:\n","\n","Every position gets a different positional vector.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$\n","PE_0=[0,1,0,1,0]\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","PE_1=[0.84,0.54,0.03,1,0]\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","PE_2=[0.91,-0.42,0.05,1,0]\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","PE_3=[0.14,-0.99,0.08,1,0]\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","PE_4=[-0.76,-0.65,0.10,0.99,0]\n","$$\n","\n","These vectors represent the positions, not the meanings of the words.\n","\n","## Sine and Cosine\n","\n","This is an important theoretical question.\n","\n","Why didn't the original Transformer use only sine? There are three main reasons.\n","\n","### Different patterns\n","\n","Sine and cosine are phase-shifted versions of each other.\n","\n","$$ \\cos(x)=\\sin(x+\\pi/2) $$\n","\n","Using both provides two complementary values for each frequency.\n","\n","### Relative position information\n","\n","The sinusoidal functions have a useful mathematical property.\n","\n","For a given frequency $\\omega$:\n","\n","$$ \\sin((pos+k)\\omega) $$\n","\n","and\n","\n","$$ \\cos((pos+k)\\omega) $$\n","\n","can be expressed using the sine and cosine values at $pos$.\n","\n","Using angle addition:\n","\n","$$ \\sin(a+b)=\\sin(a)\\cos(b)+\\cos(a)\\sin(b) $$\n","\n","$$ \\cos(a+b)=\\cos(a)\\cos(b)-\\sin(a)\\sin(b) $$\n","\n","This means a fixed positional offset can be represented through a transformation of the original positional values.\n","\n","It helps the model learn relationships between relative positions.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- cat is 1 position after The.\n","- sat is 1 position after cat.\n","- sat is 2 positions after The.\n","\n","### Different frequencies\n","\n","- Different dimensions encode positional patterns at different scales.\n","- Some dimensions change rapidly, while others change slowly.\n","- Combining them helps represent both short-range and long-range positional information.\n","\n","## Types of positional Encoding\n","\n","Sinusoidal positional encoding is not the only approach. There are several important approaches used in Transformer architectures.\n","\n","### Sinusoidal positional encoding\n","\n","Uses fixed sine and cosine mathematical functions.\n","\n","- Original Transformer approach.\n","- No trainable positional parameters.\n","- Position vectors are calculated mathematically.\n","\n","![Screenshot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY/main/images/screenshot.png)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":15,"id":"87564822","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.38775Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.38734Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.396429Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.395168Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.018721,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.398554+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.379833+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","import torch\n","import torch.nn as nn\n","import math\n","\n","class SinusoidalPositionalEmbedding(nn.Module):\n","\n","    def __init__(self, d_model, max_seq_len=5000):\n","        super().__init__()\n","\n","        pe = torch.zeros(max_seq_len, d_model)\n","\n","        position = torch.arange(\n","            max_seq_len,\n","            dtype=torch.float\n","        ).unsqueeze(1)\n","\n","        div_term = torch.exp(\n","            torch.arange(0, d_model, 2).float()\n","            * (-math.log(10000.0) / d_model)\n","        )\n","\n","        pe[:, 0::2] = torch.sin(position * div_term)\n","\n","        pe[:, 1::2] = torch.cos(\n","            position * div_term[:pe[:, 1::2].shape[1]]\n","        )\n","\n","        pe = pe.unsqueeze(0)\n","\n","        self.register_buffer(\"pe\", pe)\n","\n","    def forward(self, x):\n","\n","        seq_len = x.size(1)\n","\n","        return x + self.pe[:, :seq_len, :]\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"14dca76d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005618,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.410094+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.404476+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Learned positional encoding\n","\n","Position vectors are stored in a trainable embedding table.\n","\n","- Position 0 has a trainable vector.\n","- Position 1 has another trainable vector.\n","- The model learns these vectors during training.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":16,"id":"937ce016","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.423236Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.422743Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.43012Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.429045Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.016665,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.432279+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.415614+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["import torch\n","import torch.nn as nn\n","\n","class LearnablePositionalEmbedding(nn.Module):\n","    def __init__(self, max_seq_len, d_model):\n","        super().__init__()\n","\n","        self.position_embedding = nn.Embedding(\n","            max_seq_len, d_model\n","        )\n","\n","    def forward(self, x):\n","        # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_model)\n","\n","        seq_len = x.size(1)\n","\n","        positions = torch.arange(\n","            seq_len, device=x.device\n","        )\n","\n","        pos_emb = self.position_embedding(positions)\n","\n","        return x + pos_emb"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ee51a943","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005343,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.443063+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.43772+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Relative positional encoding\n","\n","Represents the relative distance between tokens rather than relying only on their absolute positions.\n","\n","Example: Is one token 2 positions before another?\n","\n","\n","### Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE)\n","\n","Encodes positional information by rotating components of Query and Key vectors.\n","\n","- Widely used in modern decoder-only LLMs.\n","- Incorporates relative positional information into attention.\n","\n","### ALiBi (Attention with Linear Biases)\n","\n","Adds distance-dependent biases to attention scores.\n","\n","- Does not add positional vectors to token embeddings.\n","- Encourages attention to account for token distance."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1bc76aea","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005321,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.453791+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.44847+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Combine token embeddings with positional encoding\n","\n","The input sentence is:"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":17,"id":"2a675f94","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.467408Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.466358Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.473316Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.47196Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.016572,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.475843+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.459271+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["'I love Machine Learning.'"]},"execution_count":17,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["text"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"2785ef17","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T15:32:34.660149Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T15:32:34.659535Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T15:32:34.681984Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11