,"execution_count":19,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["embeddings"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e64df323","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.006375,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.562705+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.55633+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Positional Encodings"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":20,"id":"de408b7d","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.578196Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.577757Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.63792Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.636783Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.070235,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.640255+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.57002+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["tensor([[[ 0.0000,  1.0000,  0.0000,  1.0000,  0.0000],\n","         [ 0.8400,  0.5400,  0.0300,  1.0000,  0.0000],\n","         [ 0.9100, -0.4200,  0.0500,  1.0000,  0.0000],\n","         [ 0.1400, -0.9900,  0.0800,  1.0000,  0.0000],\n","         [-0.7600, -0.6500,  0.1000,  0.9900,  0.0000]]])"]},"execution_count":20,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["pos_embedding = SinusoidalPositionalEmbedding(\n","    d_model=embedding_dim\n",")\n","\n","# Get positional embeddings\n","positional_embeddings = pos_embedding.pe[:, :embeddings.size(0), :]\n","positional_embeddings = positional_embeddings.round(decimals=2)\n","\n","positional_embeddings"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"71a0091c","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005506,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.651455+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.645949+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Final Embeddings"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":21,"id":"1e10c318","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.664897Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.664448Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.679939Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.678765Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.025404,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.682386+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.656982+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["tensor([[[ 0.3557,  1.7944,  0.6419,  1.6777,  0.7693],\n","         [ 1.1782,  0.0153,  1.8547,  0.3271, -2.7218],\n","         [-0.1314, -0.1264, -0.8534,  0.3915,  0.9904],\n","         [-1.4827, -0.7751, -0.0713,  0.8092, -1.4282],\n","         [-1.2296,  0.9194,  0.1225, -0.0335,  0.2334]]],\n","       grad_fn=<AddBackward0>)"]},"execution_count":21,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["# Add positional embeddings to token embeddings\n","final_embeddings = pos_embedding(\n","    embeddings.unsqueeze(0)\n",")\n","final_embeddings"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d0cc165f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.006416,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.695294+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.688878+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["This matrix is what gets passed to the next Transformer layer. The model now receives vectors containing both token information and positional information.\n","\n","## Sinusoidal vs Learned Positional Encoding\n","\n","Let's compare the two most fundamental approaches.\n","\n","| Feature                            | Sinusoidal               | Learned                   |\n","| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- |\n","| Position vectors                   | Mathematically generated | Learned during training   |\n","| Trainable parameters               | No                       | Yes                       |\n","| Generalization to unseen positions | Can be calculated        | Depends on implementation |\n","| Computational cost                 | Low                      | Low                       |\n","| Original Transformer               | Yes                      | No                        |\n","\n","### How does learned positional encoding work?\n","\n","Suppose your maximum sequence length is 5 and your embedding dimension is 4.\n","\n","The model maintains a positional embedding matrix:\n","\n","$$ P= \\begin{bmatrix} p_0\\\\\\ p_1\\\\\\ p_2\\\\\\ p_3\\\\\\ p_4 \\end{bmatrix} $$\n","\n","Each row contains four trainable values.\n","\n","Initially, these values are generally initialized randomly.\n","\n","During training:\n","\n","1. The model receives token IDs.\n","2. The embedding layer generates token vectors.\n","3. The positional embedding layer retrieves the corresponding position vectors.\n","4. Both vectors are added.\n","5. The model generates predictions.\n","6. The loss is calculated.\n","7. Backpropagation updates the positional embeddings along with other trainable parameters.\n","\n","Unlike sinusoidal encoding, learned positional encoding gets its positional vectors from training.\n","\n","## What happens after positional encoding?\n","\n","Now let's connect positional encoding to self-attention.\n","\n","Suppose our sentence is:\n","\n","`The cat sat on the mat.`\n","\n","After tokenization, embedding and positional encoding, we have a sequence of vectors:\n","\n","$$ X=E+PE $$\n","\n","These vectors enter the self-attention mechanism.\n","\n","Self-attention calculates three matrices:\n","\n","$$ Q=XW_Q $$\n","\n","$$ K=XW_K $$\n","\n","$$ V=XW_V $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $Q$ = Query matrix.\n","- $K$ = Key matrix.\n","- $V$ = Value matrix.\n","- $W_Q,W_K,W_V$ = Trainable weight matrices.\n","\n","Then attention is calculated using:\n","\n","$$ Attention(Q,K,V)= softmax\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V $$\n","\n","Because positional information was already added to $X$, the Query and Key representations can incorporate information about token positions.\n","\n","For example, the model can learn that:\n","\n","- `cat` is the subject of `sat`.\n","- `sat` is related to `mat`.\n","- The word `on` connects `sat` and `the mat`.\n","\n","Positional information helps the attention mechanism distinguish relationships that depend on word order.\n","\n","Important: Positional encoding does not itself determine grammar or sentence meaning. The Transformer learns those relationships during training.\n","\n","## Rotary Positional Embedding\n","\n","Unlike traditional sinusoidal encoding, which adds positional vectors to token embeddings, RoPE(Rotary Positional Embedding) applies position-dependent rotations to Query and Key vectors.\n","\n","Imagine a two-dimensional vector:\n","\n","$$ x=[x_1,x_2] $$\n","\n","A rotation by angle $\\theta$ produces:\n","\n","$$ R(\\theta)= \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\theta&-\\sin\\theta\\\\\\ \\sin\\theta&\\cos\\theta \\end{bmatrix} $$\n","\n","The rotated vector is:\n","\n","$$ x'=R(\\theta)x $$\n","\n","RoPE applies this idea to pairs of dimensions in Query and Key vectors.\n","\n","The rotation angle depends on token position and frequency.\n","\n","This means the attention calculation can incorporate relative positional relationships.\n","\n","The key distinction:\n","\n","- Sinusoidal encoding: Adds positional information to input embeddings.\n","- RoPE: Rotates Query and Key representations according to their positions.\n","\n","## Interview Questions\n","\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","Positional encoding is a technique used in Transformers to provide information about the order and position of tokens in a sequence. It is necessary because self-attention alone does not inherently represent token order.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why does a Transformer need positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","A Transformer needs positional encoding because self-attention does not inherently understand the order of tokens in a sequence. Positional encoding provides information about token positions, helping the model understand the sequence order.\n","\n","</details>\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why does the original Transformer use sine and cosine?</summary>\n","\n","The original Transformer uses sine and cosine because they generate position-dependent patterns at different frequencies. These patterns help the model distinguish token positions and understand relative positional offsets.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Is positional encoding trainable?</summary>\n","\n","It depends on the approach:\n","\n","- Sinusoidal positional encoding: Not trainable.\n","- Learned positional encoding: Trainable.\n","- RoPE: Uses predefined position-dependent rotations, although some variants introduce additional learned components.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between positional encoding and token embedding?</summary>\n","\n","Token embedding represents the semantic information (meaning) of a token, whereas positional encoding represents the position of a token in a sequence.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between absolute and relative positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","Absolute positional encoding represents the position of a token in a sequence, whereas relative positional encoding represents the distance or positional relationship between tokens.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Does positional encoding happen before or after self-attention?</summary>\n","\n","In the original Transformer, positional encoding is added to token embeddings before they enter the self-attention layers.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","# Self Attention\n","\n","Encoder Self-Attention is one of the most important mechanisms in the Transformer architecture. It allows the model to understand how each word in a sentence relates to every other word in that same sentence.\n","\n","Imagine the following sentence:\n","\n","> The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","\n","When we read this sentence, we understand that the word `it` refers to the cat.\n","\n","How?\n","\n","> Our brain considers the relationship between different words in the sentence.\n","\n","A Transformer needs a mechanism to understand these relationships.\n","\n","That's where Self-Attention comes in.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- `it` needs to pay attention to `cat`.\n","- `tired` needs to understand the context of `it`.\n","- `sat` may need to pay attention to `cat`.\n","- `mat` may relate to `sat`.\n","\n","Self-Attention allows every token to examine every other token and determine which tokens are important for understanding its meaning.\n","\n","\n","The key idea:\n","\n","Every token looks at all the tokens in the input sequence, calculates their relevance, and creates a new contextual representation of itself.\n","\n","For example, the representation of `it` will contain information about `cat`, because `cat` is relevant to understanding `it`.\n","\n","## Self Attention in Encoder\n","\n","![Self Attention](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector.png)\n","\n","The encoder generally contains these components:\n","\n","1. Input Embedding\n","2. Positional Encoding\n","3. Multi-Head Self-Attention\n","4. Add & Normalize\n","5. Feed-Forward Neural Network\n","6. Add & Normalize\n","\n","The process is:\n","\n","```\n","Input Sentence\n","      |\n","      v\n","Tokenization\n","      |\n","      v\n","Token Embeddings\n","      |\n","      v\n","Positional Encoding\n","      |\n","      v\n","Encoder Self-Attention\n","      |\n","      v\n","Add & Normalize\n","      |\n","      v\n","Feed Forward Network\n","      |\n","      v\n","Add & Normalize\n","      |\n","      v\n","Encoder Output\n","```\n","\n","Our focus is step 3: Encoder Self-Attention.\n","\n","Before self-attention begins, each token already has:\n","\n","- An embedding vector representing its learned meaning.\n","- Positional information representing its position in the sentence.\n","\n","These vectors are passed into the self-attention mechanism.\n","\n","## Components\n","\n","This is the most important concept to understand.\n","\n","Self-attention uses three vectors:\n","\n","1. Query (Q)\n","2. Key (K)\n","3. Value (V)\n","\n","Imagine you enter a library and want to find a book about Machine Learning.\n","\n","Query (Q): What am I looking for?\n","\n","Your query is:\n","\n","> I want to learn Machine Learning.\n","\n","Key (K): What information does each book advertise?\n","\n","> Each book has a title, keywords, or description.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Book A: Machine Learning Fundamentals\n","- Book B: Python Programming\n","- Book C: Database Management\n","\n","You compare your query with the information associated with each book.\n","\n","Value (V): What information does the book actually contain?\n","\n","> After determining which books are relevant, you retrieve their actual content.\n","\n","Now map this to Transformer:\n","\n","| Component | Meaning                                              |\n","| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Query     | What information is this token looking for?          |\n","| Key       | What information does this token offer for matching? |\n","| Value     | What information should this token contribute?       |\n","\n","For example, consider the token `it`.\n","\n","- Its Query asks: Which other tokens provide useful contextual information?\n","- Every token has a Key that can be compared with this Query.\n","- Every token has a Value containing information that can be combined into the final representation.\n","\n","Important: Query, Key, and Value are not manually written by developers. The Transformer learns them through trainable weight matrices."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"id":"7c2df685","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005719,"end_time":"2026-11-05T16:44:17.707442+00:00","ex